.SS Medium
The physical boot medium: bind it, back up its loader, swap, roll back.
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue dataset add <stage> <dataset>\f[R]
Record the rescue root dataset (zroot/ROOT/rescue, a boot environment on
the production pool): the stage exists, the name is a ZFS dataset name;
then written (immutable: identical re\-add is a no\-op, a differing one
is refused \(en drop first)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue transient write <stage>\f[R]
Write the transient frame into the mounted rescue root: the stage
exists, the root is mounted, the transient list is not empty; then
`stage rescue transient file' \(en the rc.d script rescue_union (from
the template), the mount point of its memory layers (/.rescue\-union),
/etc/rc.conf.d/rescue_union naming the listed paths, /etc/rc.conf.d/tmp
(tmpmfs) and /etc/rc.conf.d/var (varmfs, unless /var is itself in the
list)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue card add <stage> <medium> <partition>\f[R]
Record the rescue partition of a medium (da1p3): the stage and the
medium exist, the name is a GPT partition name; then written under
rescue/cards/<medium> (immutable, drop first to change)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue card drop <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Forget the rescue partition record of a medium: the stage exists and has
it; then it is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue dataset make <stage>\f[R]
Create the rescue root on the production pool, empty: the stage exists,
the dataset is recorded; then `stage rescue dataset create' (ONE
dataset, mountpoint / and canmount noauto \(en a boot environment the
loader can name and nothing mounts by itself; the meta package fills it)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue baseline <stage>\f[R]
Take the baseline of the rescue root \(en the manifest of its tree
(every file and symlink, LC_ALL=C sorted, `path sha256=hash' / `path
symlink=target', the format of the boot manifest) signed with the pinned
attest key, into stage/<stage>/rescue/baseline/MANIFEST(.asc): the stage
exists, the root is mounted, the key is pinned; then `stage rescue
baseline write' as root (the tree holds root\-only files) and `attest'
as the owner.
Close the root and take the snapshot right after: the snapshot and this
manifest are one image, `stage rescue verify' checks a card against it
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue passphrase <stage>\f[R]
Read the rescue passphrase hidden, twice, into
<workdir>/auth\-rescue.txt on the RAM disk (geli init \-J and geli
attach \-j take the file; it is what the owner types at the rescue
boot): the stage exists, the work directory is a mounted RAM disk; then
`stage rescue passphrase read'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue card init <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Prepare a medium\(cqs rescue partition, once: GELI (AES\-XTS 256,
HMAC/SHA256, BOOT, NODELETE, slot 0 = the rescue passphrase together
with the key file, its own master key), every sector written (HMAC needs
it), the pool created on it with ROOT below, exported again: the stage
exists, the medium is inserted, the partition, the pool and the key file
are recorded, the passphrase file is there; then `stage rescue card
geli' and `stage rescue card pool'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue card open <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Attach the medium\(cqs rescue partition (passphrase and key file) and
import its pool without mounting (altroot
$ELEBAKE_ROOT/mnt/<stage>\-rescue\-<medium>): the stage exists, the
medium is inserted, the partition, the pool and the key file are
recorded, the passphrase file is there; then `stage rescue card attach'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue card close <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Export the medium\(cqs pool and detach its rescue partition: the stage
exists, the partition and the pool are recorded; then `stage rescue card
detach'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue push <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Send the recorded snapshot to the medium\(cqs card: the stage exists,
the snapshot is recorded, the card opens (stage rescue card open), the
stream goes (the whole tree the first time, the increment since what the
card holds after), bootfs is set, the root on the card is set read\-only
(the rescue is transient), the snapshot on the card is verified against
the source, the receipt is recorded, the card closes
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue verify <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Check a card against the baseline without sending, three questions: does
the card hold the recorded snapshot (GUID) with a read\-only root, is
the baseline manifest signed by the pinned key, and is the tree on the
card exactly the baseline (every entry present and unchanged, nothing
beyond it) \(en the stage exists, the snapshot is recorded, the baseline
is there and verified, the card opens, the root is mounted read\-only,
the tree is checked, the root is unmounted, the card closes.
Anything found is an error naming it
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue package add <stage> <name>\f[R]
Record the name of the meta package that describes the rescue system
(illyria\-rescue): the stage exists, the name is a package name; then
written (immutable, drop first to change)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue package drop <stage>\f[R]
Forget the meta package name of the stage: the stage exists and has the
record; then it is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue keyfile add <stage> <path>\f[R]
Record the GELI key file of the cards as the loader names it
(/boot/keys/zroot.key, in the boot tree on the card; the host reads the
stage copy, stage/<stage>/boot<path>): the stage exists, the path is
absolute; then written (immutable, drop first to change).
The slot opens with the rescue passphrase AND this file, as zroot does
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue keyfile drop <stage>\f[R]
Forget the key file record of the stage: the stage exists and has the
record; then it is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue source add <stage> <tool> <path>\f[R]
Record the checkout a tool is packaged from (elvboot, vpn\-switch): the
stage exists, the tool is a package name, the path is absolute; then
written under rescue/sources/<tool> (immutable, drop first to change).
`stage rescue tool package' runs `make package' there
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue source drop <stage> <tool>\f[R]
Forget the checkout record of a tool: the stage exists and has the
record; then it is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue local add <stage> <line>\f[R]
Take one rc.conf line into /etc/rc.conf.local of the rescue system \(en
what the rescue deliberately keeps different from the production, whose
rc.conf travels 1:1 (sshd_enable=\(lqNO\(rq): the stage exists, the line
is name=\(lqvalue\(rq; a line already listed is a note
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue local drop <stage> <line>\f[R]
Take one line out of the rc.conf.local list: the stage exists and lists
it; then the line is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue local write <stage>\f[R]
Write /etc/rc.conf.local of the mounted rescue root from the local
lines: the stage exists, the root is mounted, the list is not empty;
then `stage rescue local file'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue user add <stage> <name>\f[R]
Take a user of this machine into the rescue system: the stage exists,
the user exists here (pw usershow); then recorded under
rescue/users/<name> as read from the production \(en uid, gid, groups,
home, shell, gecos (immutable, drop first to change).
The password hash is not a record: `stage rescue user mirror' takes it
from master.passwd when it runs as root.
`root' takes its password only
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue user drop <stage> <name>\f[R]
Forget a user record of the stage: the stage exists and has the record;
then it is removed
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue user mirror <stage>\f[R]
Create the recorded users in the mounted rescue root with the uid, gid,
groups, home and shell they have here, and copy their password hashes
(and root\(cqs) from master.passwd: the stage exists, the root is
mounted, users are recorded; then `stage rescue user write'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue tool package <stage> <tool>\f[R]
Build a tool\(cqs package from its recorded checkout into the stage\(cqs
repository THROUGH ITS PORT: the stage exists, the checkout is recorded,
the ports framework is installed (/usr/ports/Mk); then `stage rescue
tool build' \(en the distfile as a git archive of the checkout
(<tool>\-0.0.<commit count>.tar.gz into the stage\(cqs distfiles), `make
makesum package' in <checkout>/port with the stage\(cqs work directory
and repository; the meta package names the tool by the port\(cqs origin
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue package build <stage>\f[R]
Build the meta package into the stage\(cqs repository: the stage exists,
the package name is recorded, the tool list is not empty; then `stage
rescue manifest render' into +MANIFEST, `stage rescue package create'
(pkg create \-M), `stage rescue repo index' (pkg repo, and the
repository configuration the install reads)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue package install <stage>\f[R]
Install the meta package into the mounted rescue root \(en and with it
the base sets, every tool, elvboot and vpn\-switch, from this
machine\(cqs repositories and the stage\(cqs: the stage exists, the root
is mounted, the package is built; then `stage rescue package pkg'
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue patch add <stage> <name> <script> <user>\f[R]
Record a patch of the built rescue root: the stage exists, the name has
the form of a record name, the script is an absolute path IN THE IMAGE
(the package\(cqs own copy under its template tree,
e.g.\ /usr/local/lib/elebake/template/rescue/elvboot\-import\-db\-patch.sh),
the user exists on this machine; then written under
rescue/patches/<name> as `<user>:<script>' (immutable, drop first to
change) and the resource directory $ELEBAKE_BASE/.resource/<name>/ made
\(en the fixed place the owner fills with what the script takes as its
second argument (the export pair of a database: dump.sh with its .asc,
bundle.tar.gz, and signer.asc, the signer\(cqs public key).
`stage rescue patch apply' runs every patch as root INSIDE the mounted
root (a one\-shot jail): sh <script> <user> <resources>
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue patch drop <stage> <name>\f[R]
Forget a patch record of the stage: the stage exists and has it; then it
is removed (the resource directory stays: the owner\(cqs files)
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue patch apply <stage>\f[R]
Apply every recorded patch inside the mounted rescue root, in name
order: the stage exists, the root is mounted; then per patch `stage
rescue patch complete' (its script in the root, its resource directory
here) and `stage rescue patch run' (the resources hung into the root
read\-only, a one\-shot jail on the root, the script as root in it) \(en
fail\-fast, the first failing patch stops the batch.
No patch recorded is a note.
A phase of `stage rescue build', right before the baseline
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue kernel mirror <stage>\f[R]
Take the kernel of the stage\(cqs boot tree over into the mounted rescue
root: the stage exists, the root is mounted, boot/kernel/kernel is in
the stage; then `stage rescue kernel copy'.
The root runs the kernel the medium boots \(en the one the pair carries,
redacted or full \(en whether `stage include' took it from the package
under /boot or `stage build kernel' baked it from the fork; so what rc
loads at run time (kld_list, the Linuxolator\(cqs fdescfs) was built
with the kernel that runs.
The package\(cqs copy under /boot/kernel goes.
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue build <stage>\f[R]
Build the opened rescue root, in phases: the stage exists, the root is
mounted; then `stage rescue package install' (the meta package and
everything it names), `kernel mirror' (the stage\(cqs kernel over the
package\(cqs), `config mirror', `local write', `user mirror', `transient
write', `patch apply' (the databases, built into the image by the
recorded patches) and, last, `baseline' (the signed manifest of the
finished tree).
Before: dataset open and package build; after: dataset close and
snapshot
.TP
\f[B]stage rescue card rekey <stage> <medium> <old\-keyfile>\f[R]
After a rotation of the key file (zroot.key): set slot 0 of the
medium\(cqs rescue partition to the rescue passphrase and the NEW
recorded key file, opening it with the passphrase and the OLD one: the
stage exists, the medium is inserted, the partition and the key file are
recorded, the passphrase file is there; then `stage rescue card setkey'
.TP
\f[B]stage pool import <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Import the medium\(cqs pool writable, from its GPT label (confined: no
device scan), rooted under $ELEBAKE_ROOT/mnt/<stage>\-<medium>: the
description checked, then `stage pool mount' \(en idempotent, an
imported pool stays as it is
.TP
\f[B]stage pool import readonly <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Import the medium\(cqs pool read\-only (adopt reads, nothing is
written), rooted under $ELEBAKE_ROOT/mnt/<stage>\-<medium>: the
description checked, then `stage pool mount readonly' \(en idempotent
.TP
\f[B]stage pool export <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Unmount the boot dataset and export the medium\(cqs pool (the reverse of
stage pool import): the description checked, then `stage pool unmount'
\(en idempotent, a pool that is not imported is nothing to do
.TP
\f[B]stage tree sync <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Write the stage\(cqs boot/ tree onto the medium\(cqs dataset: the medium
prepared (pool imported, dataset mounted), then snapshot + copy + verify
(fail\-fast), then close (rollback if the tree is not the manifest) and
pool export \(en close and export ALWAYS run (the batch keeps going), so
the medium is never left half\-written or imported
.TP
\f[B]stage tree snapshot <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Snapshot the medium\(cqs boot dataset (elebake\-<stamp>) before it is
rewritten: the medium prepared, then `stage tree snap', then `stage tree
prune' (the older elebake\-* snapshots beyond ELEBAKE_SNAPSHOT_KEEP go)
.TP
\f[B]stage tree copy <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Replace the boot/ tree on the medium with the stage\(cqs boot/: the
medium prepared, the manifest written and attested (both idempotent),
then `stage tree write'
.TP
\f[B]stage tree verify <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Inspect the boot tree on the medium against the stage\(cqs manifest,
both directions: the medium prepared, the manifest written, the findings
taken (stage tree findings), then `stage tree matches' \(en a finding
fails the command
.TP
\f[B]stage tree close <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Leave the medium consistent: the medium prepared, the findings taken
(stage tree findings), then `stage tree judge' \(en a tree that IS the
manifest stays (the snapshot kept as history), a tree that is not goes
back to the newest elebake snapshot
.TP
\f[B]stage adopt <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Take the boot tree FROM the medium into the stage\(cqs boot/ (the
reverse direction: a card you trust becomes the description): the medium
prepared read\-only, then `stage adopt copy', then pool export
.TP
\f[B]stage device <stage> <medium> </dev/node> [<mountpoint>]\f[R]
Register a NAMED deploy medium (its backups sort under backup/<medium>):
the stage exists, the medium name and the device node are well\-formed;
then `stage medium record'
.TP
\f[B]stage boot tree <stage> <medium> <gpt\-label> <pool/dataset>\f[R]
Register where the medium\(cqs boot tree lives (used by stage push/tree
sync, adopt, pool import): `stage boot tree ensure' (the description:
stage, medium, a well\-formed label and dataset name), then the guarded
act \(en the World as it is, read here from gpart: the label names a
freebsd\-zfs partition of the medium\(cqs disk, never its EFI system
partition: `stage boot tree record', else an error line.
A medium that is not inserted is registered unchecked (a dump replays
this command; the batches that need the card check it when they run)
.TP
\f[B]stage backup <stage> <medium> [<label> [<description>]]\f[R]
Save the loader currently ON that medium as a backup RECORD
backup/<medium>/<label>/ (loader.efi, description, sha256, created,
source, by): `stage backup ensure' (the description: stage, medium, a
new record name, a description), then the acts \(en `stage medium mount'
(the card verified when it runs), `stage backup take', `stage medium
release'.
Label defaults to the UTC stamp, description to medium/stage/user
.TP
\f[B]stage backup list <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Show the backup records of that medium, oldest first (label, created,
sha256, description \(en the view for a rollback decision): the stage
and the medium exist; then `stage backup table'
.TP
\f[B]stage rollback <stage> <medium> [<label>]\f[R]
Put a backup back onto the medium \(en but FIRST save what is on the
medium now as record suspect\-<stamp> (quietly: the loader about to be
overwritten may be the evidence).
One line of preconditions, `stage rollback ensure' (the description:
stage, medium, the record present and intact), then the acts on one
mount: `stage medium mount' (the card verified when it runs), `stage
backup take', `stage rollback write' (the hash verified on the medium),
`stage medium release'
.TP
\f[B]stage deploy <stage> <medium>\f[R]
The loader swap onto the NAMED medium: the stage and the medium exist,
the device is present, the signed loader is there and newer than the
built one; then `stage deploy write' (mount, backup record of what is
there, copy, hash check, umount)
.TP
\f[B]stage medium stamp <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Stamp the medium\(cqs letter onto its ESP (, one byte): the loader reads
it as the identity of the card it was loaded from, the anchor records it
as \(lqlast booted\(rq, MediumSwitch tells when it changes \(en two
cloned cards differ in nothing else.
The stage and the medium exist, the device is present; then `stage
medium stamp write'.
Threat model assumed: the letter is an identity, not a secret; a thief
who copies a card copies the letter, and the anchor in the TPM still
says which letter booted last
.TP
\f[B]stage medium identify <stage> <medium>\f[R]
Which card is in?
The stage exists, the medium is registered, its device node is present;
then `stage medium stamp read' mounts the ESP read\-only and says the
letter the card carries (or that it carries none).
Nothing is written.
With cloned cards every registered letter names the same device node, so
any registered medium serves as the way to the node
.SS Trust foundation (catalogs and, soon, claims/gates/policies)
Toolchain readiness checks; each backend answers for itself.
.TP
\f[B]stage measure <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
List the measurements the containers\(cq catalogs offer in the
stage\(cqs checkout, with their descriptions: one `stage measure list
<container> <stage>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, or the one
container given
.TP
\f[B]stage action <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
List the actions the containers\(cq catalogs offer in the stage\(cqs
checkout, with their descriptions: one `stage action list <container>
<stage>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, or the one container given
.TP
\f[B]stage when <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
List the firing predicates the containers\(cq catalogs offer in the
stage\(cqs checkout, with their descriptions: one `stage when list
<container> <stage>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, or the one
container given
.TP
\f[B]catalog doc <file> <name>\f[R]
Print the entry\(cqs name and the description its source carries
(template/awk/catalog\-doc.awk over the catalog file); an entry without
one says so
.TP
\f[B]stage phase policy add <stage> <phase> <policy> [<position>]\f[R]
Bind a policy into a phase of the stage: ensure its prerequisites in the
container that hosts the phase, then append it to the phase\(cqs policy
list (a position inserts instead)
.TP
\f[B]stage phase policy ensure <stage> <phase> <policy>\f[R]
Unroll the containers: one `stage phase policy ensure <container>
<stage> <phase> <policy>' per container of ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS; the
container that hosts the phase ensures the policy, the others say so in
a comment line
.TP
\f[B]stage policy ensure <stage> <container> <policy>\f[R]
The policy\(cqs prerequisites in the container: every line `<kind>
<name>' of the record (`gate <gate>', `trigger <trigger>') is one `stage
<kind> ensure <stage> <container> <name>'
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation check <stage>\f[R]
The stage exists; every bound policy has its prerequisites (stage phase
policy ensure, per phase and policy); every baseline the bound policies
demand is reported when missing (gate slots, macro expectations, and
what the catalog names demand per template/tbl/baseline\-demands.tbl);
then the summary
.TP
\f[B]stage phase show <stage> [<phase>]\f[R]
List the phases the stage\(cqs checkout offers per container
(ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS, in that order), each with its bound policies: one
`stage phase show <container> <stage>' per container; one phase in
detail with the 2\-arg form
.TP
\f[B]stage phase policy drop <stage> <phase> <policy>\f[R]
Unbind a policy from a stage\(cqs phase (the policy itself survives):
the stage exists and the phase binds the policy, then the line is
removed
.TP
\f[B]macro add <MACRO> <type> <label> [<else>] [<defined>]\f[R]
Store a macro record for a compiled baseline: <MACRO> is the C macro
stem (BOARD_DIGEST), <type> sha256 | byte, <label> the measurement
label.
The 4\-arg form names the #else alternative (a verbatim C expression,
`\-' derives MEASUREMENT_NONE), the 5\-arg form also the defined name
(`\-' derives <MACRO> minus _DIGEST plus _EXPECTED).
No arsenal record is referenced: the chain is write only
.TP
\f[B]macro drop <MACRO>\f[R]
Remove a macro record: it exists, no macro expectation names the macro
it defines (a referenced record is never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]macro show [<macro>]\f[R]
Show every macro record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`macro show <macro>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]expectation add <expectation> <type> <label> <value>\f[R]
Store a named, reusable expectation: <type> byte | sha256 | string |
macro | key; a macro expectation names a macro an arsenal macro record
defines (the reference never dangles; the type word dispatches the
check); a key expectation names the leaf of a kenv record the loader
reads at run time, loader.trust.<gate>.<key> \(en for a value that
includes the loader itself (PcrBank, LoadedImages) and so cannot be
compiled into it; stage kenv learn records it, no build follows.
Only for gates of PHASE_KERNEL and later: the loader reads
loader.trust.conf after PHASE_LOADER, a key bound earlier finds no value
and the claim stays skipped.
Then the record is written \(en an identical re\-add is a no\-op, a
different one is refused (immutable; drop first)
.TP
\f[B]expectation drop <expectation>\f[R]
Remove a expectation: it exists, no claim names it as its expectation (a
referenced record is never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]expectation show [<expectation>]\f[R]
Show every expectation record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted:
one `expectation show <expectation>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]claim add <claim> <measurement> <diagnose|\-> <publish|\-> <expectation>\f[R]
Store a named, reusable claim: its expectation must exist in the arsenal
(the reference never dangles), then the record is written \(en an
identical re\-add is a no\-op, a different one is refused (immutable;
drop first)
.TP
\f[B]claim drop <claim>\f[R]
Remove a claim: it exists, no gate lists it (a referenced record is
never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]claim show [<claim>]\f[R]
Show every claim record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`claim show <claim>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]trigger add <trigger> <when> <action>\f[R]
Store a named, reusable FIRE(<when>, <action>) pair.
Each field is a catalog name or a composition without whitespace: the
when may be and(a,b,\&...), or(a,b,\&...)
or not(a), nested at will; the action may be compose(a,b,\&...)
to run several in order.
The loader sees AND/OR/NOT and COMPOSE, the sh containers { a && b; }, {
a || b; }, !
a and a; b.
The leaves are checked against the container\(cqs catalog when a policy
binds: no arsenal record is referenced, the chain is write only \(en an
identical re\-add is a no\-op, a different one is refused (immutable;
drop first)
.TP
\f[B]trigger drop <trigger>\f[R]
Remove a trigger: it exists, no policy lists it (a referenced record is
never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]trigger show [<trigger>]\f[R]
Show every trigger record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`trigger show <trigger>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]gate add <gate>\f[R]
Create a gate: <gate> is a C identifier (it lands in foundation.c).
A gate carries no secret \(en the loader compares no password, the three
factors open the encrypted providers or nothing does (the three\-factor
design).
No arsenal record is referenced: the chain is write only, a re\-add is a
no\-op and leaves the claim list untouched
.TP
\f[B]gate drop <gate>\f[R]
Remove a gate: it exists, no policy names it (a referenced record is
never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]gate claim add <gate> <claim> [<position>]\f[R]
Append a claim reference to a gate (order = evaluation order): the gate
and the claim exist (the reference never dangles), then the claim is
appended \(en a claim already listed is a no\-op; the 3\-arg form
inserts at a 1\-based position instead
.TP
\f[B]gate claim drop <gate> <claim>\f[R]
Unlink a claim reference from a gate (the claim itself survives): the
gate exists and lists the claim, then the line is removed
.TP
\f[B]gate show [<gate>]\f[R]
Show every gate record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`gate show <gate>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]policy add <policy> <gate>\f[R]
Create a named policy for a gate (triggers are appended separately): the
gate exists (the reference never dangles), then the record is written
\(en an identical re\-add is a no\-op, a different gate is refused
(immutable; drop first)
.TP
\f[B]policy drop <policy>\f[R]
Remove a policy: it exists, no phase of any stage binds it (a referenced
record is never dropped), then it is erased
.TP
\f[B]policy trigger add <policy> <trigger> [<position>]\f[R]
Append a trigger (FIRE) reference to a policy: the policy and the
trigger exist (the reference never dangles), then the trigger line is
appended \(en a trigger already listed is a no\-op; the 3\-arg form
inserts at a 1\-based position among the trigger lines instead
.TP
\f[B]policy trigger drop <policy> <trigger>\f[R]
Remove a trigger reference from a policy (the trigger itself survives):
the policy exists and lists the trigger, then the line is removed
.TP
\f[B]policy show [<policy>]\f[R]
Show every policy record (or one) as the C that WOULD be emitted: one
`policy show <policy>' per record; none is a note
.TP
\f[B]foundation dump\f[R]
Emit the foundation portion of a database dump: one block per family in
dependency order (macros, expectations, claims, triggers, gates,
policies); adds are idempotent\-immutable, so replays are safe
.TP
\f[B]foundation collect\f[R]
List the arsenal record files (macros, expectations, claims, triggers,
gates, policies) that belong into an archive
.TP
\f[B]answer hash add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <name> <hash> <policy\-template>\f[R]
One sentinel answer class in one batch: the hash is 64 hex digits and
the template policy exists; then expectation <name> (string, label
<loader\-gate>, value <hash> = sha256(salt+word) exactly as the loader
publishes loader.trust.<loader\-gate>.answer), claim <name> over
measure_answer, gate <name> with `\-' and `.' as \(cq_\(cq, policy
<name> with the triggers of the template, and the binding to <phase> of
<stage>.
Assumes the sentinel of <loader\-gate> is bound in the loader
(sentinel_act, leafs question/salt/display) and the container of <phase>
has measure_answer.
Names stay neutral (fish\-3, not fish\-halt): whoever reads the database
must not learn the class from the name
.TP
\f[B]answer add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <name> <policy\-template>\f[R]
Add one sentinel answer class with the word read HIDDEN from the
terminal when the batch runs \(en twice, both must match \(en and hashed
with the stage\(cqs salt (stage kenv add <stage>
loader.trust.<loader\-gate>.salt <hex>).
The word reaches neither argv, trace, history, the batch text nor the
generator: the running script leaves only sha256(salt+word) in a file of
the database, the next line lifts it into `answer hash add' and removes
the file.
Write the word down BEFORE typing it (inventory, paper): the database
keeps only the hash
.TP
\f[B]answer drop <stage> <phase> <name>\f[R]
Remove one answer class in reverse order: the binding in <phase>, the
policy, the gate, the claim, the expectation
.TP
\f[B]answer file add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <file>\f[R]
Roll out every answer class of a table file, one line per class `<name>
<policy\-template> <word\&...>' (the word is the rest of the line,
spaces kept; no word = the empty answer; `#' lines and blank lines
skipped): the file is readable by its owner only and the salt exists;
then per class line `answer line hash' (the word hashed when the batch
runs, into .tmp/answer/<name>) and `answer hashed add' (the hash lifted
into `answer hash add') \(en the words reach only the running script,
never argv, trace, the batch text or the generator.
The file IS the written place of the words: keep it on a tmpfs while
typing, encrypt and print it, then remove it (rm \-P)
.TP
\f[B]answer file drop <stage> <phase> <file>\f[R]
Remove every answer class named in a table file (first field of each
class line; the words are not read): one `answer drop' per line
.TP
\f[B]answer catchall add <stage> <phase> <loader\-gate> <name> <policy\-template>\f[R]
The catch\-all answer class: expectation <name> (byte, label
<loader\-gate>, 1), claim over measure_answer_matched, gate, policy with
the triggers of the template \(en a when_fail template (react\-miss:
spool\-fail, shutdown\-fail) \(en and the binding.
It fails whenever no word class matched: empty and wrong alike.
Bind it AFTER the word classes (the phase runs in binding order; a class
added later must be followed by `answer drop' + re\-add of the
catch\-all), and give every class template the trigger for
answer_matched_act
.TP
\f[B]answer show <stage>\f[R]
List the answer classes seen from the stage, in policy order: every
policy whose gate\(cqs first claim measures measure_answer (a word
class) or measure_answer_matched (the catch\-all) \(en name, the phase
of <stage> that binds it or `unbound', its triggers.
The words are not here and never were: the inventory has them
.TP
\f[B]stage earlboot mk <stage>\f[R]
Generate the earlboot rc.d script of the stage into its hooks/earlboot:
the stage and its checkout exist, the bound measurements\(cq demands on
the boot tree are met (stage require <stage> earlboot), then header,
constants, state, tools, the catalog functions, the prologue, the phases
SYSINIT and MOUNTED in that order, the footer, and the install.
Assumes: the record LOADER_TRUST_WORD_SECRET is present when a word
claim or handover is bound; mac_bootlock keeps loader.trust.* immutable;
the script is installed with `stage earlboot install' and covered by
mac_veriexec
.TP
\f[B]stage earlboot test <stage> <dump>\f[R]
Replay a captured boot through the CURRENT earlboot of the stage,
without root and without side effects: the same rendering as `stage
earlboot mk' but with mocks for state and tools \(en kenv/sysctl/kldstat
answer from <dump>, shutdown and logger become notes on stderr, the
state lives under hooks/ and is removed after \(en then the run: exit
code, every file the run left (appraisal, book, spool, marks).
Never run the INSTALLED earlboot by hand: book_act appends to the book.
Capture a dump after a boot with: { kenv; sysctl kern.osrelease
kern.ident kern.securelevel | sed `s/\(ha/sysctl./'; kldstat \-q \-m
mac_bootlock && echo `kldstat.mac_bootlock=1'; } > /tmp/ram/boot.kenv
.TP
\f[B]stage earlboot install <stage>\f[R]
Install the generated earlboot script as /etc/rc.d/earlboot (root:wheel
0500; the root dataset, so the earliest userland does not depend on a
mounted /usr/local) and enable it in /etc/rc.conf.d/earlboot; refused
while none was generated.
Run as root
.TP
\f[B]stage elvbootd mk <stage>\f[R]
Generate the elvbootd hooks of the stage into its hooks/: the stage and
its checkout exist, the bound measurements\(cq demands on the boot tree
are met, at least one runtime phase is bound; then one self\-contained
hook.<phase>.sh per BOUND runtime phase (STARTUP rc.d start, PERIODIC
periodic/security, RESUME rc.resume, MEDIA devd with $1 = the cdev,
SHUTDOWN rc.d stop), each with header, constants, state, tools, the
catalog functions (earlboot\(cqs palette inherited), the prologue and
its phase; plus the glue that plugs a hook into its mechanism:
hooks/elvbootd (rc.d) when STARTUP or SHUTDOWN is bound,
hooks/elvboot.devd.conf (devd) when MEDIA is bound
.TP
\f[B]stage elvbootd install <stage>\f[R]
Install the generated hooks under /usr/local/etc/elvboot/ (0500) and
wire each into its mechanism with the generated glue: STARTUP as
/usr/local/etc/rc.d/elvbootd (enabled), PERIODIC as
/usr/local/etc/periodic/security/900.elvboot, RESUME as a line in
/etc/rc.resume, MEDIA via /usr/local/etc/devd/elvboot.conf (devd
restarted).
Refused while nothing was generated.
Run as root
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation make <stage>\f[R]
Generate foundation.c from the bound policies into the worktree: the
stage and its checkout exist, the foundation/ directory is there, then
the sections rendered in file order into foundation.c.new (header,
macros, secrets, prerequisites, gates, phases, dispatch) and installed
as one complete file
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation <stage>\f[R]
The foundation batch: check, make, report
.TP
\f[B]stage foundation report <stage>\f[R]
Summarize the foundation of a stage: the checkout, per enum phase its
bound policies, the referenced gates and the emission target \(en notes
for the caller
.TP
\f[B]stage report <stage> [<container>]\f[R]
The one reading view of a stage: every container, every phase, the bound
policies, each policy\(cqs gate with its claims (measurement, diagnose,
publish, expectation) and its triggers in firing order (when \->
action).
Rewrites to `stage phase show <stage>'; with a container to `stage phase
show <container> <stage>'
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All configuration lives in \f[CR]ELEBAKE_*\f[R] variables, layered as
\f[CR].env/local\f[R] (override, written by \f[CR]setenv\f[R]),
\f[CR].env/default\f[R] (the installed profile) and the shipped
templates.
Interpreter pins \f[CR]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_<function>\f[R] decide per
function whether emitted shell is displayed or executed: arity\-specific
before arity\-agnostic before the class default
(\f[CR]ELEBAKE_TERMINAL_INTERPRETER\f[R], default \f[CR]cat\f[R]).
Every variable answers \f[CR]elebake help env <VAR>\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_ARCHIVE_ATTEST_KEY\f[R]
The openpgp record that signs what this database exports \(en and the
signer a receiver expects
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_CONTAINERS\f[R]
The containers a stage binds policies into, in this order: loader (the
EFI loader, phases PHASE_BOOT/LOADER/KERNEL, emitted as foundation.c),
earlboot (rc.d at mountcritlocal, SYSINIT/MOUNTED, sh), elvbootd
(runtime hooks, STARTUP/PERIODIC/RESUME/MEDIA, sh).
Each name is a catalog directory under stand/efi/loader/local of the
checkout (the loader in the headers there, the others as
<name>/{policy,measure,action}.sh with a PHASES= line).
A fourth name here is a fourth container: its own catalog directory and
phases, no code change in elebake (extensible: an intermediate layer
must be possible)
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_EDITOR\f[R]
The editor \f[CR]stage edit\f[R] opens on a file of the boot tree (stdin
from /dev/tty, so it works under \f[CR]... \(rs| sh\f[R]); edit =
FreeBSD base ee(1).
No fallback to the login environment: an unset pin is a refusal
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_FREEBSD_PREREQUISITES\f[R]
Curated tool list \f[CR]freebsd prerequisites\f[R] probes at generation
time
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_FREEBSD_SRC\f[R]
Path to the FreeBSD source repository the stages build from
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_INCLUDES\f[R]
Modules preloaded at startup (engine.sh is always loaded first)
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_catalog_doc\f[R]
Interpreter for \f[CR]catalog doc\f[R] \(en runs
template/catalog\-doc.awk over the catalog file and prints the entry
with its description
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_catalog_title\f[R]
Interpreter for \f[CR]catalog title\f[R] \(en the heading of one catalog
section (display)
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_claim_dump\f[R]
Interpreter for the dump block \f[CR]claim dump\f[R] \(en dump TEXT
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_dump_env_epilogue\f[R]
Interpreter for \f[CR]dump env epilogue\f[R] \(en dump TEXT
.TP
\f[B]ELEBAKE_INTERPRETER_dump_env_prologue\f[R]
Interpreter for \f[CR]dump env prologue\f[R] \(en dump TEXT
